Title: CLIMATE ANTHOLOGY: SEGMENT I - RISING SEAS
Credit: Written by
Author: Screenwriting Class Collective
Draft date: October 2026
Format: Fountain

/* =========================================================================
   SEGMENT I: RISING SEAS
   Setting: Low-lying delta coastal zone & urban perimeter (Khulna to Dhaka corridor)
   Themes: Land loss, salinization, internal climate refugees, urban pressure.
   Atmospheric Context: 32°C, 37.1°C Heat Index, 98% Cloud, Light Drizzle, PM2.5 21 µg/m³.
   ========================================================================= */

EXT. SUNDARBANS MANGROVE PERIMETER - DAY

A landscape of drowned silhouettes. Sundari trees stand skeletal in grey, brackish water. 

Dead crabs float belly-up in stagnant tide pools.

ANWAR (52), deep grooves weathered across his forehead, stands barefoot in ankle-deep silt. He wraps thick electrical tape around the base of a hand-pumped tubewell.

He pushes the iron handle down. 

CLANK. CLANK. A sputtering gasp of grey water trickles out.

Anwar cups the liquid in his calloused palm, raises it to his lips, and tastes it. He spits immediately onto the mud.

ANWAR
Salt. Pure salt.

Beside him, SHABNUM (22), wearing mud-splattered yellow waterproof boots and an oversized cotton tunic, writes in a water-resistant field notebook.

SHABNUM
The aquifer has been breached two miles further inland than last monsoon.

ANWAR
The rice crop in the lower polder is dead, Shabnum. If the water is brine, we aren't farming next month. We're packing.

SHABNUM
(looking towards the horizon)
To Dhaka? Like the others?

ANWAR
Dhaka doesn't have land, daughter. It only has rooms made of hot tin.

EXT. DHAKA - SADARGHAT LAUNCH TERMINAL - AFTERNOON

Dense monsoonal clouds hang like a wet mattress over the Buriganga River. 

The air is a wall of sweltering moisture: 32 DEGREES CELSIUS with a HEAT INDEX PUSHING 37. Sweat soaks every collar. 

A triple-decker river ferry GROANS against the rubber tires lining the concrete pier. A mass of PASSENGERS spills off the gangplanks:
Men balancing rolled bamboo mats; women holding bundles wrapped in plastic fertilizer bags; children clutching plastic water jugs.

RIVER HAWKER (V.O.)
(through a tinny megaphone)
Filtered water! Ten taka cold water! Clean bottle!

Shabnum steps onto the slippery dock, holding a battered leather satchel containing soil core samples. The stench of diesel exhaust, decaying river algae, and burning refuse hits her face.

She coughs, rubbing her eyes against the stinging ground-level ozone.

SHABNUM
(to herself)
Two thousand a day. Just like the reports said.

A heavy DROPLET of rain hits Shabnum's cheek. She looks up.

The sky is bruised iron. Not a drop of relief in the air.

[[WRITING PROMPT FOR CLASSMATES: Continue Shabnum's journey as she attempts to reach the National Climate Adaptation Directorate while Anwar confronts the landlord of an informal settlement in Korail.]]
